<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Augsburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Augsburg is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and was a free imperial city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. In the sixteenth century it was home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fugger-family">Fugger</a> and Welser banking families, who financed emperors including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. It also gave its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-augsburg">Peace of Augsburg</a> of 1555, which allowed princes to choose between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in their territories.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/augsburg.jpg" alt="Augsburg" width="320" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guido Radig · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augsburg_-_Markt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bavarian contingents fought in the decisive victory of Otto I over the Magyars at the Battle of Lechfeld near <b>Augsburg</b> in 955.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The court culture of the last Griffins is known through objects such as the Croÿ Tapestry of 1554, showing the dynasty with Martin Luther, and the Pomeranian Art Cabinet made in <b>Augsburg</b> for Duke Philip II, together with Eilhard Lubinus’s great map of the duchy of 1618.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Cities such as Frankfurt, <b>Augsburg</b> and Nuremberg grew wealthy as commercial entrepôts on the transit routes that crossed the empire in central Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Augsburg</b> became a textile and engineering town, Nuremberg built machines, toys and electrical equipment, and the firms that merged in 1908 as MAN supported Rudolf Diesel's development of his engine at Augsburg in the 1890s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor, won the imperial election of 1519 with money lent by the Fugger family of <b>Augsburg</b>, and Augustus II the Strong, Elector of Saxony, became a Catholic in 1697 in order to stand for the Polish throne.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>…Leonhard Kaiser, executed on 16 August 1527 (K. F. T. Schneider); that the Lutheran princes sang it entering <b>Augsburg</b> for the Diet of 1530 (Jean-Henri Merle d'Aubigné); or that it belongs to the Diet of Speyer of 1529, where the Lutheran princes protested against Emperor Charles V's wish…</blockquote><a href="